---
/**
 * How a gate runs, as a figure. The same pipeline gate/README.md draws in ASCII.
 *
 * The load-bearing claim is the split: Rust only ever extracts, and every gate is
 * a Bazel target. So a new invariant is a `.rq` file and a target, not a code
 * change — and when it fails, the rows it returns ARE the counterexample.
 */
---
<figure class="gfig pipe">
  <div class="pwrap">
    <div class="pstage">
      <div class="ptag">source</div>
      <div class="pbox">
        <code>MODULE.bazel</code><code>*.bzl</code><code>source.json</code><code>tarballs</code>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="parrow" aria-hidden="true"><span>project</span></div>

    <div class="pstage">
      <div class="ptag rust">Rust — extraction only</div>
      <div class="pbox one"><code>facts.ttl</code></div>
    </div>

    <div class="parrow" aria-hidden="true"><span>rdf_dataset</span></div>

    <div class="pstage">
      <div class="ptag bazel">Bazel — every gate is a target</div>
      <div class="pbox">
        <code class="hit">sparql_query_test(*.rq)</code>
        <code class="soon">rdf_validate_test(shapes)</code>
      </div>
      <div class="pnote">
        one target per invariant · zero rows means it holds
        <span class="soonnote">the convention tier is not built yet</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="parrow" aria-hidden="true"><span>certify</span></div>

    <div class="pstage">
      <div class="ptag soon">planned</div>
      <div class="pbox one"><code class="soon">certificate.binpb</code></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="gcap">
    Adding an invariant is <b>one query file plus one target</b> — no Rust rebuild.
    Dashed boxes are designed, not implemented.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
